## Vercel Web Analytics Installation

Successfully installed and configured Vercel Web Analytics for the Finance-QA Next.js application.

### Changes Made

#### 1. Package Installation
- Installed `@vercel/analytics` package (latest version) via npm
- Package added to dependencies in package.json
- package-lock.json updated with new dependency tree

#### 2. Code Changes
**Modified: app/layout.tsx**
- Added import statement: `import { Analytics } from "@vercel/analytics/next";`
- Added `<Analytics />` component at the end of the body element in RootLayout
- Placed after all other control components and children to ensure proper tracking across all pages

#### 3. Implementation Details
- Followed the official Vercel documentation (fetched from https://vercel.com/docs/analytics/quickstart)
- Used the Next.js App Router specific implementation pattern
- The Analytics component is placed in the root layout to track all pages in the application
- Import uses the framework-specific path '@vercel/analytics/next' as recommended for Next.js

#### 4. Verification
- Build completed successfully with no errors
- TypeScript compilation passed without issues
- All routes generated correctly (/, /_not-found, /review, /session-expired)
- No lint errors (project doesn't have ESLint configured)
- No test failures (project doesn't have tests configured)

#### 5. Next.js Environment Update
**Modified: next-env.d.ts**
- Auto-generated file updated by Next.js during build
- Changed import paths from `.next/dev/types/` to `.next/types/` (standard Next.js behavior)

### Usage Notes
- Analytics will start tracking once the application is deployed to Vercel
- Verify setup by checking browser Network tab for requests to `/<unique-path>/view` after deployment
- Enable Web Analytics in the Vercel dashboard for the project to see data
- The Analytics component is lightweight and won't impact page load performance

### Files Modified
1. app/layout.tsx - Added Analytics component and import
2. package.json - Added @vercel/analytics dependency
3. package-lock.json - Updated dependency lock file
4. next-env.d.ts - Auto-updated by Next.js build process
